Q1. File System Space Management:

Objective: Monitor disk usage and automatically delete or rotate/compress log files when disk utilization exceeds 80%.

Pseudocode:
BEGIN
INPUT disk_partition
SET threshold = 80
SET critical_threshold = 90
WHILE system_is_running DO
    disk_usage = GET_DISK_USAGE(disk_partition)
    IF disk_usage >= threshold THEN
        PRINT "Warning: Disk usage is above 80%"
        log_files = GET_LOG_FILES()
        SORT log_files BY age DESCENDING
        FOR each log_file IN log_files DO
            IF disk_usage < threshold THEN
                BREAK
            ENDIF
            IF log_file IS CURRENTLY_IN_USE THEN
                ROTATE log_file
            ELSE
                COMPRESS log_file
            ENDIF
            disk_usage = GET_DISK_USAGE(disk_partition)
        ENDFOR
        IF disk_usage >= critical_threshold THEN
            PRINT "Critical disk usage"
            FOR each log_file IN log_files DO
                IF disk_usage < threshold THEN
                    BREAK
                ENDIF
                IF log_file IS NOT CURRENTLY_IN_USE THEN
                    DELETE log_file
                    disk_usage = GET_DISK_USAGE(disk_partition)
                ENDIF
            ENDFOR
        ENDIF
    ENDIF
    WAIT for monitoring_interval
ENDWHILE
PRINT "Disk monitoring stopped"
END

OUTPUT: Disk utilization is maintained below the specified threshold while protecting currently active log files.








Q2. Priority-Based CPU Scheduling:

Objective: Improve CPU efficiency by selecting processes according to their priority while preventing low-priority processes from being ignored indefinitely.

Pseudocode:
BEGIN
INPUT process_list
FOR each process IN process_list DO
    INPUT process.priority
    INPUT process.burst_time
    SET process.remaining_time = process.burst_time
    SET process.waiting_time = 0
END FOR
SORT process_list BY priority DESCENDING
WHILE process_list IS NOT EMPTY DO
    SELECT process WITH HIGHEST priority
    PRINT "Executing Process:", process.ID
    ALLOCATE CPU TIME to process
    EXECUTE process FOR time_quantum
    process.remaining_time 
        process.remaining_time - time_quantum
    IF process.remaining_time <= 0 THEN
        PRINT "Process completed:", process.ID
        REMOVE process FROM process_list
    ELSE
        REDUCE process.priority SLIGHTLY
        FOR each other_process IN process_list DO
            INCREASE other_process.priority SLIGHTLY
        END FOR
        RE-SORT process_list BY priority DESCENDING
    ENDIF
ENDWHILE
PRINT "All processes completed"
END

Output: CPU is allocated according to priority while priority adjustment helps prevent starvation of lower-priority processes.








Q3. RAM Resource Management for Virtual Machines:

Objective: Distribute available RAM among multiple virtual machines while reserving memory for the host operating system.

pseudocode:
BEGIN
INPUT total_RAM
INPUT host_reserved_RAM
INPUT number_of_VMs
SET minimum_RAM = 2 GB
SET available_RAM = total_RAM - host_reserved_RAM
IF available_RAM <= 0 THEN
    PRINT "No RAM available for virtual machines"
    END
ENDIF
FOR i = 1 TO number_of_VMs DO
    IF available_RAM >= minimum_RAM THEN
        ALLOCATE minimum_RAM TO VM[i]
        available_RAM = available_RAM - minimum_RAM
        PRINT "Allocated", minimum_RAM, "to VM", i
    ELSE
        PRINT "Insufficient RAM for VM", i
        SET VM[i] = "Not Allocated"
    ENDIF
END FOR
IF available_RAM > 0 THEN
    remaining_VMs = COUNT of allocated VMs
    IF remaining_VMs > 0 THEN
        extra_RAM = available_RAM / remaining_VMs
        FOR each allocated_VM DO
            ALLOCATE extra_RAM TO allocated_VM
        END FOR
    ENDIF
ENDIF
PRINT "RAM allocation completed"
PRINT "Remaining RAM:", available_RAM
END

Output: RAM is distributed among VMs while maintaining the minimum allocation and preserving host memory.








Q4. Hypervisor Selection:

Objective: Select the most appropriate hypervisor based on performance, hardware access, budget, and system requirements.

pseudocode:
BEGIN
INPUT performance_required
INPUT budget
INPUT hardware_access
INPUT number_of_VMs
INPUT isolation_required
IF hardware_access = TRUE AND performance_required = HIGH THEN
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 1 Hypervisor"
    reason = "Provides high performance and direct hardware access"
ELSE IF performance_required = HIGH AND number_of_VMs > 5 THEN
    IF hardware_access = TRUE THEN
        selected_hypervisor = "Type 1 Hypervisor"
        reason = "Suitable for high-performance multi-VM environments"
    ELSE
        selected_hypervisor = "Type 2 Hypervisor"
        reason = "Hardware access is limited"
    ENDIF
ELSE IF budget = LOW THEN
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 2 Hypervisor"
    reason = "Lower cost and suitable for desktop/testing environments"
ELSE IF isolation_required = HIGH THEN
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 1 Hypervisor"
    reason = "Provides strong isolation and efficient resource management"
ELSE
    selected_hypervisor = "Type 1 Hypervisor"
    reason = "Provides better overall resource efficiency"
ENDIF
PRINT "Selected Hypervisor:", selected_hypervisor
PRINT "Reason:", reason
END

Output: The algorithm selects Type 1 or Type 2 according to the given system requirements and constraints.








Q5. CPU and Memory Resource Limiting Using cgroups:

Objective: Control CPU and memory usage of applications to prevent one application from consuming excessive system resources.

pseudocode:
BEGIN
INPUT application_list
FOR each application IN application_list DO
    CREATE cgroup FOR application
    INPUT cpu_limit
    INPUT memory_limit
    SET CPU_LIMIT of cgroup = cpu_limit
    SET MEMORY_LIMIT of cgroup = memory_limit
    ASSIGN application PROCESS to cgroup
END FOR
WHILE system_is_running DO
    FOR each application IN application_list DO
        cpu_usage = GET_CPU_USAGE(application)
        memory_usage = GET_MEMORY_USAGE(application)
        IF cpu_usage > cpu_limit THEN
            THROTTLE CPU usage of application
            PRINT "CPU usage limited for", application
        ENDIF
        IF memory_usage > memory_limit THEN
            LIMIT memory allocation
            IF memory_usage remains above limit THEN
                TERMINATE or RESTART application
                PRINT "Memory limit exceeded by", application
            ENDIF
        ENDIF
    END FOR
    WAIT for monitoring_interval
ENDWHILE
PRINT "Resource management completed"
END

Output: CPU and memory consumption of applications are controlled using resource limits, improving overall system stability and efficiency.
